## First real use case

Early in the project we only had an "add product" module. As the business evolved, video products were split out from multi-image products. The pain points:

1. Every version change meant editing both modules at once
1. Lots of duplicated logic and methods

## Choosing an approach

Given that, the options I considered:

1. Extract shared logic
1. Extract a shared container component
1. Mixins
1. Higher-order components

**Shared logic extraction:** the shared code included state, props, and `setState`. Pulling it out meant rewiring a lot of state handling — high risk of breaking existing modules.

**Shared container:** merge the common parts of both modules, split upload into multi-image and video components, and switch type via props. That also meant merging routes and adding type params — high change cost.

**Mixins:** the approach has known flaws (details elsewhere).

The best remaining option was higher-order components: low invasiveness, functional thinking (same input, same output, strong predictability).

## Concepts

### Higher-order functions

A function is higher-order if it meets at least one of:

- Takes one or more functions as input
- Returns a function

### Higher-order components

By analogy: an HOC takes a component, does work with it inside a function, and returns a new component.

## Reading HOCs

Example 1 (props proxy)

```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
      return <WrappedComponent {...this.props}/>
    }
  }
}
```

Example 2 (inheritance inversion)

```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends WrappedComponent {
    render() {
      return super.render();
    }
  }
}
```

What? Props proxy, inheritance inversion — inheritance is already a lot, and now *inverted*? Hang on.

### Props proxy

In short: wrap the component and work with props. This nested-component / pass-through pattern is already familiar in everyday React.

Code:

```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
      return <WrappedComponent {...this.props}/>
    }
  }
}

class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        //....
    }
}

// using the HOC
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```

### Manipulating props

```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
        const newProps = {
            name: "HOC"
        }
      return <WrappedComponent {...this.props} {...newProps}/>
    }
  }
}

class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        //....
    }
}

// using the HOC
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```

If you have passed props into components, you have basically done this.

### refs

```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
      return <WrappedComponent ref={this.onRef} {...this.props}/>
    }
  }
}
```

Also familiar — calling methods on a component instance.

### Abstracting state

```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    onChange = (data = {}) => {
        this.setState(data)
    }
    render() {
        const {name = ''} = this.state;
        const newProps = {
            name: {
                value: name,
                onChange: (e)=>this.onChange({name: e.target.value})
            }
        }
        return <WrappedComponent {...this.props} {...newProps}/>
    }
  }
}

class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        const {name} = this.props;
       return <input {...name} />
    }
}

// using the HOC
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```

Sit with this pattern. When you need to drive lots of form fields, you will see why it helps.

At first I did not get what "abstract state" meant. Writing these notes made it click: operations that used to live as dynamic `state` assignment in the original component get lifted into the HOC and passed down as props.

**Summary:** props proxy feels a lot like normal component writing — with a few extra tricks.

### Inheritance inversion

Look at this again:

```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends WrappedComponent {
    render() {
      return super.render();
    }
  }
}
```

`super.render()` is the ES6 class pattern of `super.method()` — using it here is clever.

From the code, we inherit the wrapped component, and `super.render()` runs that component's `render` — i.e. renders its UI. That is where it gets interesting.

### Working with state and props

Working with state I understood. Working with props felt weird — until I saw code like this:

```plain text
// example from a React HOC getting-started guide on Juejin
const HOCFactoryFactory = (...params) => {
    // can tweak params here
    return (WrappedComponent) => {
        return class HOC extends Component {
            render() {
                return <WrappedComponent {...this.props} />;
            }
        }
    }
}

HOCFactoryFactory(params)(WrappedComponent)

// familiar pattern: redux
connect(params)(Index)
```

Then it made sense.

### Render hijacking

Someone else's code:

```plain text
// from 《深入React技术栈》

const HOC = (WrappedComponent) =>
    class extends WrappedComponent {
        render() {
            const elementsTree = super.render();
            let newProps = {};
            if (elementsTree && elementsTree.type === 'input') {
                newProps = {value: 'may the force be with you'};
            }
            const props = Object.assign({}, elementsTree.props, newProps);
            const newElementsTree = React.cloneElement(elementsTree, props, elementsTree.props.children);
            return newElementsTree;
    }
}
class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        return(
            <input value={'Hello World'} />
        )
    }
}
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```

Roughly like operating on the DOM (my mental model). I have not needed this yet, so I will not expand.

That covers the common HOC patterns. Back to the real project: how I used them.

A few numbers:

**Before refactor:**

Multi-image product module: ~900 lines. Video product module: ~1000 lines.

**After:**

Multi-image: 554 lines. Video: 650 lines.

Extracted HOC: 387 lines.

That is as far as my skills got me.

I went with inheritance inversion. Props proxy can work with props but not truly operate on state — people may say "but you can abstract state," and yes: abstract, not operate.

Props proxy also cannot use the original component's static methods. There is a library, `hoist-non-react-statics`, that batch-copies statics.

The approach that let me operate on both props and especially state was inheritance inversion.

That is as far as I have studied and practiced for now. If I learn more, I will keep updating.
